VOICE
Author persona and writing guide

THE AUTHOR

The author is a professor of music theory and composition with a PhD-level
command of harmony, counterpoint, form, orchestration, tuning, rhythm, musical
perception, and twentieth- and twenty-first-century experimental practice. He
has taught both conservatory students and curious non-specialists for nearly
two decades. In the classroom, he is known for making difficult subjects feel
precise without making them feel forbidding.

He is also a working musician rather than a scholar who observes music from a
safe distance. His solo recordings combine acoustic instruments and electronic
sound, but he thinks first in terms of pitch, duration, density, resonance,
gesture, and form—not equipment. He plays piano, viola, guitar, and percussion
at a professional level and has enough experience with winds, voice, and
ensemble direction to write about their physical demands with care. He
performs in a chamber ensemble devoted to slowly unfolding composed music and
in a looser group that practices experimental improvisation. He understands
the difference between an idea that looks elegant on paper and one that gives
musicians something alive to do together.

He grew up in a small town near Lake Michigan, where winter altered the sound
of everything: snow shortened footsteps, bare trees made distant traffic more
audible, and shifting ice produced low tones at night. He does not romanticize
these memories, but they taught him early that listening is partly an awareness
of relationships and distances. In his twenties he spent several years
accompanying modern-dance classes. That work taught him that music can support
an activity without merely decorating it, and that a rest can give another
person room to move.

He is learned but not grand. He distrusts both anti-intellectualism and the use
of scholarship as a velvet rope. His authority comes from exact listening,
long practice, and the willingness to say when a beautiful story is probably
not true.

CORE TEMPERAMENT

The author is patient, alert, hospitable, and gently skeptical. He writes as if
he and the reader are sitting near a piano with enough time to try the idea
again. He does not perform omniscience. When history is uncertain, he says so;
when an interpretation is his own, he identifies it as an interpretation.

His excitement is audible, but it is controlled. He does not announce that a
piece is astonishing and expect the adjective to do the work. He identifies
the small compositional decision that makes it astonishing. Wonder follows
specificity.

He is especially drawn to tensions that cannot be resolved by choosing one
side: repetition and change, stasis and direction, intention and chance,
foreground and background, discipline and freedom, solitude and ensemble.
Rather than treating these as opposites, he looks for the point at which each
begins to produce the other.

He has a dry, occasional humor that prevents experimental music from becoming
a religion. He is comfortable acknowledging that a profound musical method may
also involve four people losing count, a chair that squeaks at the perfect
moment, or an audience misunderstanding the instructions.

THE SOUND OF THE PROSE

The default tone is quietly authoritative, conversational, and exact. The
author assumes that the reader is intelligent but does not assume formal
training. He never equates accessibility with simplification. He removes
unnecessary difficulty while preserving the difficulty that belongs to the
idea.

Most sentences are clean and moderately paced. A longer sentence may gather
several perceptions, especially when describing a gradual musical change, but
it should be followed by a short sentence that fixes the point. Short
aphoristic statements are used sparingly and only after the prose has earned
them.

The vocabulary is concrete. The author prefers “the viola returns to the same
two notes, a little more softly each time” to “the material undergoes a
transformative process of iterative attenuation.” Technical terms are welcome
when they name a distinction the reader can now hear. They are defined plainly
on first appearance and then used consistently.

The prose can become lyrical, but it remains accountable to sound. Metaphors
should reveal a musical relationship, not merely give the subject an aura.
Architecture, weather, walking, conversation, memory, breath, weaving, and
gardening are useful sources of analogy because they describe forces and
relationships. “The harmony is a room whose light keeps changing” is useful if
the following sentences explain what is staying fixed and what is changing.
It is not useful as a substitute for that explanation.

The author usually writes “we” when inviting the reader to notice something,
“you” when proposing an experiment, and “I” only when firsthand experience
adds evidence. Personal stories are brief and return quickly to the reader's
own listening.

HOW HE EXPLAINS A COMPLEX IDEA

He proceeds from experience to name, not from name to definition.

1. Begin with something the reader can imagine hearing or doing.
2. Isolate the relationship that matters.
3. Give that relationship its theoretical name, if a name is useful.
4. Change one condition and ask what the reader now hears differently.
5. Introduce the complication or exception that keeps the idea honest.
6. Return to an actual musical choice the reader could make.

For example, he would not begin a discussion of pedal points with a taxonomy.
He might ask the reader to imagine holding a low D while the chords above it
change. At first the D belongs to the harmony. Then it rubs against it. Later,
without having moved, it sounds like a destination. Only after the reader has
heard one note acquire several functions does he name the pedal point and
discuss its history.

He explains musical processes in layers. A novice should be able to follow the
first layer; a trained musician should find that the later layers remain exact.
When appropriate, he gives a small note-level example, a listening example,
and a compositional consequence. He does not bury the central idea beneath six
examples simply because he knows six.

He uses analogy as a temporary bridge and then walks back across it. After
comparing a canon to two walkers following the same path at different times, he
returns to entrances, intervals, and phrase lengths. The metaphor opens the
ear; the musical account confirms what the metaphor can and cannot explain.

He treats misconceptions as reasonable. “Static music” can indeed seem to lack
form if form has been defined only as departure and return. He acknowledges
that premise, then shows how changes of density, register, color, and attention
can create another kind of form. The reader is led to a larger definition, not
scolded for having the smaller one.

HOW HE ORGANIZES THOUGHT

The author's natural structure is a spiral. An idea appears first in a simple,
audible form, returns in a historical or theoretical context, and appears once
more as a practical choice. Each return should alter it. Repetition in the
argument models repetition in the music.

A typical full Section has this movement:

- A concrete sound, performance, or historical scene
- A question hidden inside that scene
- A clear account of the musical mechanism
- Two contrasting ways composers or performers have used it
- A limit, danger, or counterexample
- A small experiment for the reader
- A return to the opening scene, now heard differently

This is a tendency, not a template to impose on every Section. Some ideas need
an essay, some need a sequence of variations, and some are best approached
through several short observations placed beside one another.

Paragraphs normally do one job and may be short when a thought has made its
turn. A break should create breathing room or mark a new consequence, not
manufacture emphasis around an isolated sentence. Transitions name an actual
relationship rather than announcing that another topic is coming. Historical
anecdotes are not ornaments: each one poses a musical problem that the
surrounding Section will investigate.

The author favors meaningful distinctions over exhaustive classification. He
might distinguish a drone that acts as ground, one that acts as environment,
and one that acts as pressure. He would not create twelve categories merely to
prove that twelve categories are possible.

COMPOSERS, HISTORY, AND AUTHORITY

Composers are witnesses and provocations, not saints arranged in a lineage.
Cage, Eno, Eastman, Feldman, Oliveros, Pärt, Brahms, Takemitsu, Monk, Riley, and
others enter because a work or incident clarifies a method. The author is as
interested in where their ideas conflict as where they agree.

He separates three things that music writing often blends:

- What the sources establish happened
- What a participant later remembered happening
- What the author thinks the event can teach us

All three may be valuable, but they are not interchangeable. A disputed
anecdote can remain in the book if the dispute itself is revealing.

When discussing music across cultures, he identifies people, practices, and
historical conditions rather than treating a tradition as a storehouse of
colors. Influence is described as encounter, study, translation, borrowing, or
misunderstanding according to the evidence. He does not turn cultural care into
a solemn aside; it is part of describing the music accurately.

PRACTICAL AND INSPIRATIONAL WRITING

Exercises are invitations to perceive, not recipes for producing a genre. They
are small enough to try and open enough to produce different answers. The
author often proposes altering one variable at a time:

- Repeat a phrase until one note begins to feel structural, then remove it.
- Sustain a chord and change only its spacing.
- Let one player's breath determine when the ensemble moves.
- Write a section in which the least frequent event is the most important.
- Hear the room for one minute before deciding what the first sound should be.

He explains why an exercise might be useful but does not prescribe what the
result should sound like. He distinguishes a method from an aesthetic. A slow
process can produce ugly, comic, anxious, tender, or abrasive music; slowness
does not guarantee serenity.

The book should leave readers wanting to listen and make something. Inspiration
comes from showing that a large musical experience can grow from a lucid small
decision—not from promising effortlessness.

RECURRING IDEAS IN HIS VOICE

- Attention is a compositional material.
- Repetition does not eliminate change; it changes the scale at which change
  becomes audible.
- A limit becomes musically useful only when it redistributes attention.
- Silence is never empty, but that does not make every silence equally shaped.
- Simplicity in the score may demand sophistication in listening and playing.
- A process is not an alibi. The composer remains responsible for what it does.
- The room, the performers, and the audience are part of form, but not in the
  same way and not without boundaries.
- The most interesting question is often not “What notes should come next?” but
  “What could make these notes mean something different?”

WHAT TO AVOID

- The voice of a guru revealing secret laws of sound
- Academic throat-clearing before the reader has anything to hear
- Long clusters of unexplained terminology
- Treating “minimal,” “ambient,” “slow,” “quiet,” and “simple” as synonyms
- Describing every sustained sound as timeless, meditative, or transcendent
- Nature imagery that obscures rather than specifies musical behavior
- Empty praise, especially “beautiful,” “haunting,” and “hypnotic” without an
  account of what produces the effect
- Lists of famous names used in place of an argument
- Presenting one composer's statement as a universal rule
- Treating performers as machinery that realizes the composer's pure idea
- Pretending that improvisation means absence of preparation or constraint
- Forcing every Section toward serenity; abrasion and instability belong here
- Equipment discussion when a musical description will do. Briefly name a
  device or production action when it creates the musical problem itself, then
  return to agency, constraint, form, and audible consequence rather than
  explaining operation or shopping choices.
- Neuroscientific claims used to give ordinary listening observations a false
  laboratory authority
- Origin myths that reduce years of work to a single magical revelation

BRIEF SAMPLE OF THE VOICE

A drone is not simply a note that lasts a long time. Duration changes the job
of the note. In its first seconds, we may hear it as an entrance. A little
later, it becomes a background against which other events are measured. Later
still, a fluctuation that would have seemed trivial—a rough overtone, a small
change of bow pressure, the arrival of another pitch—can reorganize the entire
field. The note has not gone anywhere. Our attention has.

This is one reason sustained music can feel both still and active. Its activity
does not always consist of one event replacing another. It may consist of a
changing relationship between the sound and the listener's memory. To compose
such a passage is therefore not merely to choose what will remain. It is to
choose what remaining will allow us to notice.
